  ;; Help
  :action/help     "Help"
  :help/title      "Help"
  :help/q-header   "What is a header row?"
  :help/a-header   "The first line of many CSV files names the columns — id, name, city — rather than holding data. Ticking the box tells this application to treat it that way, so it is not counted as a row and can be repeated at the top of every file it makes."
  :help/q-quoted   "One of my cells contains a line break. Is that a problem?"
  :help/a-quoted   "No. A value wrapped in quotation marks may contain line breaks, and this application keeps such a row whole. Splitting a file by counting lines, as many tools do, would cut that row in half and quietly damage both pieces."
  :help/q-damaged  "Why does it say some rows look damaged?"
  :help/a-damaged  "Because they do not follow the usual rules — a row with more columns than the first one, or a stray quotation mark. Nothing is repaired or thrown away: damaged rows are copied across exactly as they arrived, so you lose nothing and can look at them yourself."
  :help/q-encoding "What is text encoding?"
  :help/a-encoding "It is how letters are stored as numbers in the file. This is worked out for you and normally needs no attention. The one sign that it went wrong is accented characters looking like nonsense when you open a result, and the setting under Advanced is there for that case."
  :help/q-excel    "Why does it mention 1,048,576 rows?"
  :help/a-excel    "That is the most rows Excel can open in one sheet. Older versions stopped at 65,536. If a file exceeds the limit Excel will not open it at all, which is usually the reason for splitting the file in the first place."
  :help/q-replace  "What happens to files already in the folder?"
  :help/a-replace  "Nothing, unless you say so. Before writing anything, the folder is checked for files this split would take the names of. If any exist you are shown the list and asked, and you can write somewhere else instead."

